1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,680
Plans don't fail at planning.

2
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,760
They fail at the first surprise.

3
00:00:17,140 --> 00:00:22,150
PlanB schedules a real dinner: owners, dependencies, equipment, a budget —

4
00:00:22,410 --> 00:00:26,170
checked by a constraint engine, and re-checked by an independent validator.

5
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:53,370
Now the surprise: guests an hour early, and no oven.

6
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:55,970
PlanB first shows what it understood —

7
00:00:56,180 --> 00:00:58,120
nothing moves until you confirm.

8
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:46,210
Every change is explained by the constraint that forced it.

9
00:01:46,620 --> 00:01:48,850
New finish: five fifty-five —

10
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:51,010
five minutes before the guests walk in.

11
00:02:09,570 --> 00:02:11,580
A second shock, absorbed.

12
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:15,240
And the plan remembers — this session survives a reload.

13
00:02:31,880 --> 00:02:34,830
And when no plan exists, PlanB says so —

14
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:37,670
it never invents time, people, or money.

15
00:02:37,990 --> 00:02:40,850
In its own audit: zero broken constraints.

16
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:44,940
PlanB Orchestra — a simulated Alexa+ experience.
